Bootstrap: docker
From: nvidia/cuda:12.6.3-cudnn-devel-ubuntu24.04

# Protenix v1 -- AlphaFold3-class structure prediction with covalent bonds.
#
# REWRITTEN 2026-09-25 after the first build attempt failed, and both reasons
# it failed are recorded here rather than quietly fixed, because each one would
# have produced a WORKING-LOOKING image that was wrong.
#
# ── 1. The base had no nvcc, and Protenix compiles CUDA at IMPORT ──────────
#
# The first recipe used python:3.12-slim with build-essential, reasoning by
# analogy with boltz_2.def: a C compiler covers Triton's JIT. That analogy is
# wrong here. `protenix/model/layer_norm/layer_norm.py` runs, at module import
# and with NO fallback path:
#
#     try:    fast_layer_norm_cuda_v2 = importlib.import_module(...)
#     except ImportError:
#             fast_layer_norm_cuda_v2 = compile(name=..., sources=[
#                 "layer_norm_cuda.cpp", "layer_norm_cuda_kernel.cu"], ...)
#
# A `.cu` source needs **nvcc**, not cc. The torch wheel carries a CUDA runtime
# and no toolkit, so `protenix --help` died in %test -- which is the good case.
# The bad case is that the build's %post never imports the model modules, so an
# image built without %test would have shipped, passed `import protenix`, and
# failed on the first GPU job.
#
# So: a -devel base, for nvcc. This is the same conclusion intellifold_2.def and
# openfold3_3.def reached from upstream's documentation; this entry reached it
# from a failure, which is the weaker way to learn it and the one that produces
# a number.
#
# CUDA 12.6 and not 12.8, deliberately: `pip install protenix` resolves
# torch 2.7.1+cu126, and the extension is compiled against THAT torch's headers.
# A 12.8 toolchain over a cu126 torch is a version skew with no upside here,
# because this image has no sm_120 kernels anyway -- see GPU_MAX_CC below.
#
# ── 2. `--upgrade` resolved PROTENIX 2.0.0, which is the version this entry
#       exists to refuse ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# tool.conf's first paragraph says, in capitals, that this entry is pinned at
# v1 because the v2 WEIGHTS carry no open-source licence and restrict the
# outputs -- and then the recipe said `pip install --upgrade protenix`, which
# on 2026-09-25 installs 2.0.0. PyPI carries 1.0.0 ... 1.1.0 and then 2.0.0;
# the major version tracks the model generation. The policy was in a comment
# and the command contradicted it, which is invariant #5's failure mode wearing
# a different hat: the thing that decides is not the thing anybody reads.
#
# It is pinned now, exactly, and a version bump is a reviewed edit to this line.
#
# ── 3. Nothing JIT-compiles at run time, because %post already did ─────────
#
# layer_norm.py prefers its OWN directory when that is writable and falls back
# to torch's extension cache when it is not. site-packages is writable during
# %post and read-only in the finished .sif, so importing the model modules here
# bakes the compiled extension into the image. Two things follow: the compile
# is proven at build time rather than discovered on a compute node, and a job
# never writes a CUDA build tree at all.
#
#
# ══ REBUILT 2026-09-24 (protenix_2): TEMPLATES ═════════════════════════════
#
# protenix_1.sif could reach the template code and could never use a template.
# Three things were wrong and all three are fixed here. Each was MEASURED in
# protenix_1.sif, not read off a README.
#
# ── 1. No kalign, and Ubuntu has no package for it ────────────────────────
#
# Protenix re-aligns every template hit against the query with kalign, and
# throws away whatever alignment the .a3m or .hhr carried. Its own config says
#
#     "kalign_binary_path": "/usr/bin/kalign",  # apt-get install kalign
#
# and that advice does not work on this base: kalign is in Debian, and is in
# NO Ubuntu 24.04 component -- not main, not universe. `apt-cache policy
# kalign` returns nothing at all. So it is built from source here, v3.4.0,
# pinned by tag and sha256, installed with prefix /usr so that it lands on the
# exact path Protenix already defaults to and NO config patch is needed for it.
#
# Version note, because the registry now contains two and they are not
# interchangeable: chai1_2.def installs kalign 3.x from Debian because chai_lab
# passes `--nthreads`, which kalign 2.04 rejects. Protenix passes the older
# `-format fasta`, which kalign 3.4.0 still accepts -- measured -- so 3.x is a
# superset and both tools get the same major version. bioconda's `kalign` is
# still 2.04; do not substitute it here to "match upstream".
#
# ── 2. The inference template date cutoff was unreachable ─────────────────
#
# This is the one worth reading. `infer_dataloader.py` constructed the
# featurizer with a STRING LITERAL:
#
#     max_template_date="2021-09-30",
#
# which is Protenix v1's training cutoff. Every structure released after it was
# rejected. That is correct for benchmarking and wrong for prospective work,
# and ADR 0020 in the consuming project admits a template ONLY if it postdates
# the cutoff -- so the two conditions were exactly complementary and nothing
# was admissible.
#
# It is not a policy. It is a one-line bug, and upstream's own code says so:
#
#   * `TemplateHitFilter._is_after_cutoff` opens with `if cutoff is None:
#     return False` -- a designed "no cutoff" state.
#   * `TemplateHitFeaturizer.get_templates` takes a per-call
#     `max_template_date` override.
#   * and the inference call site in `template_featurizer.py` ALREADY passes
#     `max_template_date=None`, plainly meaning "do not date-filter here".
#
# That None is dead, because `get_templates` tests it with `if
# max_template_date:` and falls back to the constructor's literal. The
# intention is in the source and the truthiness test defeats it.
#
# The patch below does NOT change that shared function and does not touch the
# training path. It makes the ONE literal read a config key whose default is
# the same literal, so upstream behaviour is bit-identical unless the key is
# set. The key is environment-driven, which is the idiom `configs_data.py`
# already uses for PROTENIX_ROOT_DIR:
#
#     PROTENIX_MAX_TEMPLATE_DATE=3000-01-01
#
# 3000-01-01 is not invented here either -- it is the sentinel Protenix's own
# training branch uses for "no cutoff" a dozen lines away in
# `template_featurizer.py`.
#
# ── 3. fetch_remote defaults TRUE, and compute nodes have no internet ─────
#
# `configs.data.template.get("fetch_remote", True)` -- so a template whose
# mmCIF is not already staged is fetched from PDBe mid-job. On this cluster
# that is a hang, not an error (doc 02). The key did not exist in the config
# dict at all, so it could not be turned off. It exists now, same env idiom:
#
#     PROTENIX_TEMPLATE_FETCH_REMOTE=false
#
# ── What is NOT patched, and why it matters ───────────────────────────────
#
# The COVALENT bond path needed nothing. `covalent_bonds` is read by
# `json_to_feature.py:add_bonds_between_entities` and works, for a CCD ligand
# by atom name and for a SMILES ligand by RDKit atom-map number. It was never
# a template blocker and the two were never related.
#
# Two prefilter gates also survive on purpose, because they are real science
# rather than defects, and the consuming project has to plan around them:
#
#   * a hit whose ungapped sequence is a substring of the query AND covers
#     >95% of it is dropped as a DuplicateError -- so the exact reference
#     structure of a target cannot template itself here;
#   * a hit shorter than 10 residues is dropped as a LengthError -- so a
#     peptide chain cannot be templated at all.
#
# Both are warnings, not errors, under the default strict_error_check=False,
# which means they drop the template and the fold still exits zero.
#
# Build:  chpc-tool build protenix        (login node only; --fakeroot is
#                                          unavailable here and `module load
#                                          apptainer` does not exist -- it is
#                                          /usr/bin/apptainer, 1.5.2)

%labels
    Tool         protenix
    Version      1
    Upstream     https://github.com/bytedance/Protenix
    UpstreamRef  protenix==1.1.0 (PyPI)
    Licence      Apache-2.0 (code and v1 model parameters)
    Base         nvidia/cuda:12.6.3-cudnn-devel-ubuntu24.04
    Kalign       3.4.0, built from source (no Ubuntu package exists)
    Patched      template date cutoff + fetch_remote made config keys
    Maintainer   Craik Lab, UCSF

%post
    set -eux
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
    apt-get update
    # ubuntu24.04 ships python3.12. python3-venv because this distribution
    # marks its interpreter externally-managed and `pip install` into it
    # refuses; a venv is the supported answer and --break-system-packages is
    # not one. build-essential for the C++ half of the extension -- nvcc drives
    # a host compiler and does not replace it.
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        python3 python3-dev python3-venv build-essential git ca-certificates \
        cmake curl
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

    python3 -m venv /opt/venv
    # On PATH for the REST OF %post, not merely called by absolute path. The
    # second rebuild died on `RuntimeError: Ninja is required to load C++
    # extensions` with ninja installed, because torch's cpp_extension loader
    # verifies it by running `ninja --version` as a SUBPROCESS -- importable is
    # not enough, it has to be findable -- and pip had put it in /opt/venv/bin,
    # which nothing had added to PATH. Same shape as the %environment entry
    # below, and the reason that one exists.
    export PATH=/opt/venv/bin:$PATH
    /opt/venv/bin/pip install --no-cache-dir --upgrade pip
    # PINNED. See the header: `--upgrade` here installed 2.0.0, whose weights
    # this entry forbids.
    #
    # `ninja` is NOT decoration and is NOT a dependency of protenix 1.1.0 --
    # 2.0.0 happens to pull it in and 1.1.0 does not, which is one more way the
    # unpinned install was quietly different. torch's cpp_extension loader
    # refuses without it:
    #
    #   RuntimeError: Ninja is required to load C++ extensions
    #
    # and it refuses at the same import that compiles the fused LayerNorm, so
    # on a -slim image with no build step this would have been a run-time
    # failure on a GPU node rather than a build-time one here.
    /opt/venv/bin/pip install --no-cache-dir ninja protenix==1.1.0

    # --- kalign 3.4.0, from source -------------------------------------------
    # Not apt: kalign is in no Ubuntu 24.04 component (see the header). Prefix
    # /usr, NOT /usr/local, so the binary lands on /usr/bin/kalign -- the exact
    # path protenix's own configs_data.py already defaults kalign_binary_path
    # to. Installing it anywhere else would mean patching that too, and a patch
    # avoided is a patch that cannot drift.
    KALIGN_VERSION=3.4.0
    KALIGN_SHA256=67d1a562d54b3b7622cc3164588c05b9e2bf8f1a5140bb48a4e816c61a87d4a8
    # NOT /tmp, and this is not fastidiousness. apptainer binds the HOST /tmp
    # into the build, this login node's /tmp is shared with every other user,
    # and python puts the working directory first on sys.path -- so a build that
    # cd's to /tmp and then runs python imports whatever another user happens to
    # have left lying there. This recipe already died once that way: a stray
    # /tmp/inspect.py from an unrelated project shadowed the standard library
    # and `import torch` failed with somebody else's FileNotFoundError. Build in
    # a directory this image owns, and leave it before running anything.
    mkdir -p /opt/src
    cd /opt/src
    curl -fsSL -o kalign.tar.gz \
        "https://github.com/TimoLassmann/kalign/archive/refs/tags/v${KALIGN_VERSION}.tar.gz"
    echo "${KALIGN_SHA256}  kalign.tar.gz" | sha256sum -c -
    tar xzf kalign.tar.gz
    mkdir -p "kalign-${KALIGN_VERSION}/build"
    cd "kalign-${KALIGN_VERSION}/build"
    cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr ..
    make -j"$(nproc)"
    make install
    cd / && rm -rf /opt/src
    # Assert the PROPERTY: not that the binary exists, but that it accepts the
    # command line protenix's aligner actually builds, and returns an alignment.
    test -x /usr/bin/kalign
    /usr/bin/kalign --version
    mkdir -p /opt/katest && cd /opt/katest
    printf '>a\nMKTAYIAKQRQISFVKSHFSRQLEERLGLIEDSEATKG\n>b\nMKTAYIAKQRQISFVKSHFRQLEERLGLIE\n' > ka.fa
    /usr/bin/kalign -i ka.fa -o ka.afa -format fasta
    test -s ka.afa
    grep -q '^MKTAYIAKQRQISFVKSHF' ka.afa
    cd / && rm -rf /opt/katest

    # --- RECORDED PATCHES to the installed protenix ---------------------------
    # Two of them, both about templates, both explained at length in the header.
    # They are applied by a script that ASSERTS its target text first, so a
    # version bump that moves either line fails THIS BUILD loudly rather than
    # matching nothing and shipping an image where the fix silently is not in.
    # That is the whole reason this is not a sed one-liner.
    /opt/venv/bin/python - <<'PATCHPY'
import pathlib

SITE = pathlib.Path("/opt/venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages")


def patch(path, old, new):
    p = SITE / path
    text = p.read_text()
    if new in text:
        raise SystemExit(f"{path}: already patched -- refusing to double-apply")
    n = text.count(old)
    if n != 1:
        raise SystemExit(
            f"{path}: expected exactly 1 occurrence of the target text, found {n}.\n"
            f"Upstream moved it. Re-read the source and update this recipe;\n"
            f"do NOT relax the match.\n--- looked for ---\n{old}"
        )
    p.write_text(text.replace(old, new))
    print(f"patched {path}")


# 1. Two new template config keys, environment-driven, upstream defaults kept.
#    configs_data.py already reads os.environ for PROTENIX_ROOT_DIR, so this is
#    that file's own idiom rather than a new mechanism.
patch(
    "configs/configs_data.py",
    '        "kalign_binary_path": "/usr/bin/kalign",  # apt-get install kalign\n'
    "    },\n",
    '        "kalign_binary_path": "/usr/bin/kalign",  # built from source, see protenix_2.def\n'
    "        # --- added by corehpc protenix_2.def ---------------------------\n"
    "        # Upstream hard-coded the inference template cutoff and had no key\n"
    "        # for it. Default is the literal it replaced, so behaviour is\n"
    "        # unchanged unless PROTENIX_MAX_TEMPLATE_DATE is set. 3000-01-01 is\n"
    "        # protenix's own no-cutoff sentinel (template_featurizer.py).\n"
    '        "max_template_date": os.environ.get(\n'
    '            "PROTENIX_MAX_TEMPLATE_DATE", "2021-09-30"\n'
    "        ),\n"
    "        # Upstream default is True: an unstaged template mmCIF is fetched\n"
    "        # from PDBe mid-job, which on a compute node with no route out is a\n"
    "        # hang. The key did not exist, so it could not be turned off.\n"
    '        "fetch_remote": os.environ.get(\n'
    '            "PROTENIX_TEMPLATE_FETCH_REMOTE", "true"\n'
    '        ).strip().lower() not in ("0", "false", "no", "off"),\n'
    "    },\n",
)

# 2. The one literal. Note this touches ONLY the inference dataloader; the
#    training path in template_featurizer.py keeps its own dates untouched.
patch(
    "protenix/data/inference/infer_dataloader.py",
    '                max_template_date="2021-09-30",\n',
    "                max_template_date=configs.data.template.get(\n"
    '                    "max_template_date", "2021-09-30"\n'
    "                ),\n",
)
PATCHPY

    # Which architectures the fused kernel is built for, declared rather than
    # inherited. torch guesses from the visible devices, and the login node has
    # none, so an unset list makes the build depend on a default that has moved
    # between torch releases. 8.9 is the L40S and 9.0 the H100 NVL, which are
    # the two device classes this image may be scheduled on (the RTX PRO 6000
    # at 12.0 is excluded by GPU_MAX_CC, because this torch has no sm_120).
    export TORCH_CUDA_ARCH_LIST="8.9;9.0"
    export MPLCONFIGDIR=/tmp/mpl

    # Assert PROPERTIES, not imports (tools/README.md, "Assert the property you
    # care about"). placer's recipe is the cautionary case: `import torch`
    # passed while conda had swapped torch for a CPU build, and nothing noticed
    # until a GPU job ran at CPU speed.
    /opt/venv/bin/python - <<'PY'
import sys, torch
assert sys.version_info[:2] == (3, 12), sys.version

# A CUDA build, not a CPU one. torch.cuda.is_available() is the WRONG check
# here: the login node has no GPU, so it is False for a perfectly good image.
# torch.version.cuda is a property of the WHEEL and answers the question we
# actually have.
assert torch.version.cuda is not None, "CPU-only torch -- this image cannot fold anything"
print("python", sys.version.split()[0], "torch", torch.__version__, "cuda", torch.version.cuda)

import protenix
print("protenix", getattr(protenix, "__version__", "?"))

# The arch flags ARE the GPU_MAX_CC measurement, and this is the cheapest
# moment to take it. _cuda_getArchFlags reads the compiled-in list out of the
# C extension and needs no GPU -- unlike torch.cuda.get_arch_list(), which
# returns [] when no device is visible, which is exactly the login node.
flags = torch._C._cuda_getArchFlags()
print("arch flags:", flags)
sm = [int(t[3:]) for t in (flags or "").split() if t.startswith("sm_") and t[3:].isdigit()]
if sm:
    top = max(sm)
    print("GPU_MAX_CC candidate: %d.%d" % (top // 10, top % 10))
    print("  -> copy that into tools/protenix/tool.conf, replacing UNMEASURED.")
    if top >= 120:
        print("  -> sm_120 present: safe on every gpu node, bare --gres=gpu:1 is fine.")
    else:
        print("  -> NO sm_120: needs --gres=gpu:nvidia_l40s:1 or an H100, never a bare --gres=gpu:1.")
else:
    print("NO sm_ arch flags -- this torch has no CUDA kernels at all. Do not publish.")
    raise SystemExit(1)
PY

    # Compile the fused LayerNorm INTO the image, and prove it landed in
    # site-packages rather than in a per-user cache that the .sif will not
    # carry. This import is the one that failed the first build.
    /opt/venv/bin/python - <<'PY'
import os
from protenix.model.layer_norm import layer_norm as ln

module = ln.fast_layer_norm_cuda_v2
where = getattr(module, "__file__", "")
print("fused layer norm:", where)
assert "/opt/venv/" in where, (
    "the CUDA extension compiled somewhere outside the image "
    f"({where!r}); a .sif is read-only, so every job would recompile it on the "
    "compute node -- which is the failure this build exists to move forward."
)
assert os.path.exists(where)
PY

    # The console script is what a job actually runs, and it is what failed
    # last time. Exercise it here, not only in %test.
    /opt/venv/bin/protenix --help > /dev/null
    command -v nvcc
    command -v cc || command -v gcc

%environment
    export LC_ALL=C.UTF-8
    export PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1
    export PATH=/opt/venv/bin:$PATH
    # A .sif is a read-only squashfs. Anything that JIT-compiles and caches
    # beside its own source refuses at IMPORT time inside one -- boltz hit this
    # with numba, four seconds in, before any weights or GPU. Point the usual
    # caches at the host's /tmp, which apptainer binds, is local NVMe on every
    # compute node, and is per-node so two jobs cannot fight over one file.
    #
    # TORCH_EXTENSIONS_DIR and MPLCONFIGDIR are on this list because the first
    # build proved they belong on it: torch's extension cache defaulted under a
    # read-only path and raised `OSError: [Errno 30] Read-only file system`
    # during import, and matplotlib printed the same complaint about
    # /root/.config one line earlier. The extension is compiled into the image
    # above, so this is insurance rather than the mechanism.
    export NUMBA_CACHE_DIR="${NUMBA_CACHE_DIR:-/tmp/numba-cache}"
    export TRITON_CACHE_DIR="${TRITON_CACHE_DIR:-/tmp/triton-cache}"
    export TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR="${TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR:-/tmp/inductor-cache}"
    export TORCH_EXTENSIONS_DIR="${TORCH_EXTENSIONS_DIR:-/tmp/torch-extensions}"
    export MPLCONFIGDIR="${MPLCONFIGDIR:-/tmp/mpl}"
    # PROTENIX_ROOT_DIR is deliberately NOT set here. It has to point at this
    # cluster's FAC share, which is a site fact and not a property of Protenix;
    # the registry owns site facts and chpc-tool exports it from CHPC_REFDATA.
    # Baking a share path into the image makes the image wrong for the next lab
    # that builds from this recipe.

%test
    set -eu
    # Against the BUILT, read-only image. %post runs on a writable overlay, so
    # an import that only fails on squashfs cannot be caught there -- that is
    # how boltz's numba failure got past a green build (doc 07).
    python -c "import protenix, torch; print('protenix ok, torch', torch.__version__)"
    # The import that failed the first build, on the read-only filesystem this
    # time. If the extension were not inside the image this raises OSError
    # rather than recompiling, because there is nowhere to write.
    python -c "
from protenix.model.layer_norm import layer_norm as ln
print('fused layer norm ok:', ln.fast_layer_norm_cuda_v2.__file__)
"
    protenix --help > /dev/null
    # nvcc has to be reachable from the RUNTIME environment, not merely present
    # during %post: a rebuild that slims the base would otherwise only be
    # noticed by a GPU job.
    sh -c 'command -v nvcc >/dev/null' || { echo "no nvcc on PATH in the built image"; exit 1; }
    sh -c 'command -v cc >/dev/null || command -v gcc >/dev/null' \
      || { echo "no C compiler on PATH in the built image"; exit 1; }

    # --- templates: the aligner, the key, and the gate it opens ---------------
    # All three assert a PROPERTY. "kalign exists" and "the key is present" are
    # both true of an image where templates still silently do nothing, which is
    # exactly the state protenix_1.sif was in.
    # Run from / and never from the host's /tmp: python puts the working
    # directory first on sys.path, this cluster's /tmp is shared, and %post
    # already died once because another user's inspect.py was sitting in it.
    cd /
    command -v kalign
    kalign --version
    # The command line protenix's own aligner builds, not a generic one.
    KT=$(mktemp -d)
    printf '>a\nMKTAYIAKQRQISFVKSHFSRQLEERLGLIEDSEATKG\n>b\nMKTAYIAKQRQISFVKSHFRQLEERLGLIE\n' > "$KT/kt.fa"
    kalign -i "$KT/kt.fa" -o "$KT/kt.afa" -format fasta
    test -s "$KT/kt.afa"
    rm -rf "$KT"

    # Upstream default preserved when the environment says nothing. If this
    # fails, the patch changed behaviour for everyone rather than making it
    # selectable, which is not what it is allowed to do.
    python -c "
from configs.configs_data import data_configs
t = data_configs['template']
assert t['max_template_date'] == '2021-09-30', t['max_template_date']
assert t['fetch_remote'] is True, t['fetch_remote']
print('template config defaults preserved')
"
    # And that the environment actually moves it.
    PROTENIX_MAX_TEMPLATE_DATE=3000-01-01 PROTENIX_TEMPLATE_FETCH_REMOTE=false python -c "
from configs.configs_data import data_configs
t = data_configs['template']
assert t['max_template_date'] == '3000-01-01', t['max_template_date']
assert t['fetch_remote'] is False, t['fetch_remote']
print('template config override works')
"
    # The literal is gone from the inference path. A patch that matched nothing
    # would leave this string in place, and every later assertion would still
    # pass, so check the file itself.
    python -c "
import pathlib, protenix
p = pathlib.Path(protenix.__file__).parent / 'data' / 'inference' / 'infer_dataloader.py'
src = p.read_text()
assert 'max_template_date=\"2021-09-30\"' not in src, 'the hard-coded cutoff is STILL in infer_dataloader.py'
assert 'configs.data.template.get(' in src, 'the patch did not land'
print('inference cutoff now reads the config key')
"
    # The behaviour the whole patch exists for: a 2024 structure is rejected
    # under the training cutoff and accepted under the sentinel. This is
    # upstream's own filter, called directly.
    python -c "
from datetime import datetime
from protenix.data.template.template_utils import TemplateHitFilter
f = TemplateHitFilter(release_dates={'8udr': datetime(2024, 10, 16)}, obsolete_pdbs={})
assert f._is_after_cutoff('8udr', datetime(2021, 9, 30)) is True, 'expected rejection under the training cutoff'
assert f._is_after_cutoff('8udr', datetime(3000, 1, 1)) is False, 'expected acceptance under the sentinel'
assert f._is_after_cutoff('8udr', None) is False, 'None must mean no cutoff'
print('date gate opens and closes as intended')
"

%runscript
    exec protenix "$@"

%help
    Protenix v1 -- AlphaFold3-class structure prediction, pinned at v1 because
    the v2 WEIGHTS carry no open-source licence and restrict the outputs. The
    pip version is pinned too, at 1.1.0: PyPI's `protenix` major version tracks
    the model generation and `--upgrade` installs 2.0.0.

      chpc-tool exec protenix -- protenix pred -i in.json -o out \
          -n protenix_base_default_v1.0.0 -s 101 -e 5 < /dev/null

    The subcommand is `pred` (`predict` does not exist), the model is named
    explicitly, and `< /dev/null` matters: kalign reads stdin and a job whose
    stdin is an open pipe hangs until its time limit (tools/protenix/README.md,
    Traps). Until 2026-09-26 this text said `protenix predict --input`; the
    image built 2026-09-24 still shows that line under `apptainer run-help`.

    This image has NO sm_120 kernels (torch 2.7.1+cu126 builds sm_50 .. sm_90),
    so ask for --gres=gpu:nvidia_l40s:1 or an H100, never a bare --gres=gpu:1.

    Weights and the chemical component dictionary live on FAC under
    PROTENIX_ROOT_DIR, not inside the image and not on scratch. Fetch them once
    from the LOGIN node with tools/protenix/fetch-weights.sh: compute nodes have
    no internet, so a job that has to download is a job that hangs until it
    times out.

    Covalent bonds: a top-level covalent_bonds list of
      {entity1, copy1, position1, atom1, entity2, copy2, position2, atom2}
    where entityN is the 1-BASED POSITION IN THE sequences LIST, not a chain id.
    Reorder that list and every bond moves without a word. A SMILES ligand's
    atom is either a 0-based index into the SMILES string or an
    element-plus-ordinal name (C3, N2) whose ordinal is 1-based.

    MSAs: pairedMsaPath / unpairedMsaPath, absolute paths. Generate them on the
    login node; a compute node cannot reach any MSA server.
